# Git repository cleaner

`cleaner git` replaces error-prone `filter-branch` recipes with
`git-filter-repo`, keeps normal garbage collection conservative, and exposes
unreachable objects before removing them.

The `tool` is `git`; choose an `--operation`, optionally point `--repo` at
another work tree, and use `--backup` to override the rewrite bundle path.

## Operations

### `audit`

Runs `git count-objects -vH` and a full `git fsck --unreachable --no-reflogs`.
It prints object metadata only, not object contents.

### `gc`

Expires unreachable reflog entries and runs `git gc`. The default
`--expire 30.days.ago` retains recent recovery points; use another Git
approximate date when needed. `--expire now` and zero-duration dates are
rejected unless `--force` is also selected.

### `dangling`

Shows every unreachable object and a dry-run reflog expiry before applying.
Immediate and zero-duration expiry is rejected unless `--force` is selected.

### `rewrite`

Requires `git-filter-repo` and either `--path` or `--replace-text`. A
`--path` is removed from every commit by default; select `--keep-path` to keep
only that path. Before rewriting, the command creates a bundle containing every
ref. Rewrites change commit IDs and can remove the `origin` remote.

## Direct CLI

```sh
cleaner git audit
cleaner git gc --expire 90.days.ago
cleaner git dangling --expire 30.days.ago
cleaner git rewrite --path path/to/secret.env
cleaner git rewrite --replace-text replacements.txt
```

Nothing mutates without `--apply`. Direct applies require typing `APPLY`; the
shared TUI confirms with `CLEAN` and then supplies `--apply --yes`.
